{"t":20402,"dir":"out","frame":{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"Every evening at dusk, Teodor climbed the one hundred and twelve iron steps of the Cape Vell light, his knees complaining at each landing. He had kept the lamp for thirty-one years, long enough to know the sea's moods better than his own.\n\nThe light had been automated a decade ago. The government sent a letter, polite and final, and a young engineer who pressed a button to show him the new system. Teodor nodded and said nothing. The machine did not need him. But every night he still climbed, wound the clockwork that turned the lens, and wiped the glass with a cloth that smelled of linseed oil.\n\nNobody ever asked him why. His daughter in the city called it sentimental. The fishermen who still passed by simply lifted a hand.\n\nOne November storm, the radio went silent at midnight. The automated lamp flickered, then died. Teodor, awake in his chair, heard the wind change pitch and knew at once what it meant. He pulled on his oilskins and climbed.\n\nThe lamp room was black and freezing. He carried fuel to the old generator by hand, primed it with trembling fingers, and coaxed it to life. Then he turned the lens by hand, the old way, a slow circle of gold that swept across the water.\n\nFar out, a single lantern bobbed. Then another. Then a third, turning toward the rocks, toward the gap in the reef that only the light had ever shown.\n\nThey came in one by one, guided safely home.\n\nAt dawn the storm broke. Teodor sat on the gallery rail, hands raw, watching the whole fleet unload at the harbor below. His daughter's message arrived that afternoon: Are you alright? The news had told her everything.\n\nHe typed one stiff-fingered reply: The light was needed."}},"session_id":"00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001","parent_tool_use_id":null,"uuid":"00000000-0000-4000-8000-00000000002a"}}
